comments.body:
This struct represents two possible outcomes, the tx was found or it wasn't, but the type doesn't keep its fields consistent: it can hold a `tx` together with `pruned_block_hashes`, or a `block_hash` without a `tx`. My proposal is to use `std::variant<TxFound, TxMiss>` rather than one struct where the outcome is implied by which fields are set.


 For example,[ `wallet::TxState` in `src/wallet/transaction.h`](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/5c726f2058a4d5ac2c7d0dc8753d4ff125d03f15/src/wallet/transaction.h#L81) models a transaction's state the same way, as a variant of structs.



I suggest something like:

```cpp
/// A transaction found in the mempool or in a block.
struct TxFound {
    CTransactionRef tx;
    /// Null if the transaction was found in the mempool.
    uint256 block_hash;
};

/// A lookup that did not find the transaction.
struct TxMiss {
    /// Blocks that may contain the transaction but were pruned. They may be false positives.
    std::vector<uint256> pruned_block_hashes;
};
```

